<span class="kicker">2026-07-28 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Make a Curved Paver Walkway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>A curved walkway does more for a yard’s overall feel than a straight one ever will. It slows down the eye, works with existing landscaping instead of cutting through it, and often just fits the natural shape of a yard better than a rigid straight line would. The tradeoff is that curves are genuinely harder to execute well. Every paver along the curve needs some adjustment, and small errors that would be invisible on a straight walkway become obvious once you’re following an arc.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we build curved walkways across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, and this is one of the more technically demanding walkway styles we install. This guide covers the two main ways to create a curve, cutting pavers to follow a precise arc, or using smaller units and pattern choices to create a curved look with less cutting, along with the layout, base, and cost considerations specific to curved designs.</p>
<h2><b>Two Ways to Build a Curved Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Approach</b></td><td><b>Best For</b></td><td><b>Cutting Required</b></td><td><b>Visual Result</b></td></tr><tr><td>Cut pavers to follow the arc</td><td>Precise, tight curves</td><td>High</td><td>Clean, exact curve line</td></tr><tr><td>Smaller pavers or curve-friendly patterns</td><td>Gentle, sweeping curves</td><td>Low to moderate</td><td>Slightly more organic, less exact edge</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Cutting standard rectangular pavers to follow an arc gives you the most control over exactly where the curve sits, which matters for tighter curves or when the walkway needs to follow a specific path around a fixed feature like a tree or garden bed. Using smaller pavers, like cobble-style units, or choosing a pattern that naturally accommodates gentle curves reduces the amount of cutting significantly, but works best on broader, more relaxed curves rather than tight turns.</p>
<h2><b>Planning Your Curve Before You Touch a Shovel</b></h2>
<h3><b>Lay Out the Curve With a Flexible Line First</b></h3>
<p>Before any digging happens, mark your intended curve using a garden hose or a length of rope laid directly on the ground. This lets you walk the line, view it from different angles, including from inside the house or from the street, and adjust the shape before it becomes permanent. A curve that looks right on paper often needs tweaking once it’s actually laid out in three dimensions.</p>
<h3><b>Avoid Curves That Are Tighter Than They Need to Be</b></h3>
<p>A gentle, sweeping curve is both easier to build and generally more visually pleasing than a tight, aggressive one. If your layout has a curve radius smaller than roughly 3 feet, expect significantly more cutting and a more technically demanding installation. Where possible, widen the curve’s radius slightly rather than forcing a tight turn, unless the layout is working around a fixed obstacle that requires it.</p>
<h3><b>Account for Extra Material</b></h3>
<p>Curved walkways use more pavers per square foot of actual walking surface than straight ones, since cut pieces generate waste and the outer edge of a curve covers more ground than the inner edge for the same walkway width. Budget for 15 to 20 percent extra material on a curved design, higher than the 5 to 10 percent typically set aside for a straight walkway.</p>
<h2><b>Building a Curved Walkway by Cutting Pavers to the Arc</b></h2>
<h3><b>Establish the Inner and Outer Curve Lines First</b></h3>
<p>Once your rough layout is set, mark the precise inner and outer edges of the curve using stakes and a flexible line, spaced at consistent intervals matching your walkway’s width. This gives you two clear boundary lines to work within as you lay and cut pavers.</p>
<h3><b>Dry Lay Before Cutting Anything</b></h3>
<p>Place full, uncut pavers along the curve first, without adhesive or final positioning, to see how they naturally sit against the arc. This dry layout reveals exactly where gaps will need cut pieces and roughly what size those cuts need to be, before you commit to cutting a single paver.</p>
<h3><b>Cut Pavers Individually to Fill Gaps</b></h3>
<p>Each gap along a curve is typically a slightly different size and angle, which means cutting pavers one at a time to fit its specific space rather than cutting a batch of identical pieces in advance. Mark each cut directly against the gap it’s filling, then cut with a paver saw for the cleanest edge. This is the most time-consuming part of a curved installation, and it’s where rushing tends to produce a visibly uneven curve line.</p>
<h3><b>Keep Joint Width Consistent Around the Curve</b></h3>
<p>Joint spacing naturally wants to pinch tighter on the inside of a curve and stretch wider on the outside. Pay close attention to keeping joints within a reasonable, consistent range on both sides, since joints that vary too dramatically along the curve are one of the more noticeable signs of an inexperienced installation.</p>
<h2><b>Building a Curved Walkway With Curve-Friendly Patterns</b></h2>
<h3><b>Use Smaller Format Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Smaller pavers, like cobble-style units roughly 4 by 4 inches or smaller, naturally follow a gentle curve with far less cutting than larger rectangular pavers, since more, smaller units can shift slightly at each joint to accommodate the arc without needing precise cuts.</p>
<h3><b>Choose a Pattern That Bends Naturally</b></h3>
<p>Certain patterns lend themselves to curves better than others. A running bond pattern using smaller units can sweep along a gentle curve reasonably well, while herringbone and basketweave are considerably harder to bend around a curve without extensive cutting, since their interlocking geometry assumes straight, consistent rows.</p>
<h3><b>Accept a Slightly More Organic Edge</b></h3>
<p>This approach trades some precision for speed and reduced material waste. The outer edge of the walkway will read as a smooth curve overall, but won’t have the exact, crisp line that comes from individually cut pavers. For many yards, especially those with a more relaxed, garden-path feel, this reads as an appropriate stylistic choice rather than a shortcut.</p>

